The table structure information is (table names): 
['circuits', 'constructor_results', 'constructor_standings', 'constructors', 'driver_standings', 'drivers', 'lap_times', 'pit_stops', 'qualifying', 'races', 'results', 'seasons', 'status', 'sprint_results', 'short_grand_prix_names', 'short_constructor_names', 'liveries', 'tdr_overrides', 'circuits_ext', 'constructors_ext', 'drivers_ext', 'driver_standings_ext', 'lap_times_ext', 'lap_time_stats', 'races_ext', 'team_driver_ranks', 'drives', 'retirements', 'lap_positions']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and how they can help  

• races.year – season that every race belongs to; used to group by season.  
• races.race_id – primary key that lets us join any “ *standings” table to the calendar.  
• races.round – lets us pick the last round of every season (the cumulative standings after that race equal the final totals).  
• driver_standings.race_id – join‐key to races so we can know the year of the standings row.  
• driver_standings.driver_id – identifies the driver whose points we are looking at.  
• driver_standings.points – cumulative points total after the referenced race.  
• drivers.driver_id – join‐key to look‑up a person’s name.  
• drivers.forename / drivers.surname / drivers.full_name – the textual name we ultimately want to return.  
• constructor_standings.race_id – join‐key to races for year/round information.  
• constructor_standings.constructor_id – identifies the team.  
• constructor_standings.points – cumulative constructor points after the race.  
• constructors.name – the constructor’s full name to report.  

Typical conditions that will be needed  

1. Pick only the last race of every season:  
   WHERE r.round = ( SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year )  

2. After that, rank competitors by points inside every season:  
   ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.year ORDER BY ds.points DESC) = 1  

Below are ten exploratory SQL statements (all capped at 20 rows) that progressively build toward the final result.  

```sql
-- 1. Distinct list of seasons available in the dataset
SELECT DISTINCT r.year
FROM races AS r
ORDER BY r.year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Inspect how driver standings link to races (year) and points
SELECT ds.driver_id,
       r.year,
       ds.points
FROM driver_standings AS ds
JOIN races AS r ON r.race_id = ds.race_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. For each year, find the highest round number (= last GP of the year)
SELECT r.year,
       MAX(r.round) AS max_round
FROM races AS r
GROUP BY r.year
ORDER BY r.year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Driver standings rows that correspond only to the final race of each year
SELECT r.year,
       ds.driver_id,
       ds.points
FROM driver_standings AS ds
JOIN races AS r  ON r.race_id = ds.race_id
WHERE r.round = (
        SELECT MAX(r2.round)
        FROM races AS r2
        WHERE r2.year = r.year
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Rank those final‑race driver totals and keep the #1 per season
SELECT *
FROM (
    SELECT r.year,
           ds.driver_id,
           ds.points,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.year ORDER BY ds.points DESC) AS rn
    FROM driver_standings AS ds
    JOIN races AS r ON r.race_id = ds.race_id
    WHERE r.round = (
            SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year
          )
) AS sub
WHERE rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Attach the driver’s full name to the champion rows
SELECT sub.year,
       d.full_name,
       sub.points
FROM (
    SELECT r.year,
           ds.driver_id,
           ds.points,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.year ORDER BY ds.points DESC) AS rn
    FROM driver_standings ds
    JOIN races r ON r.race_id = ds.race_id
    WHERE r.round = ( SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year )
) AS sub
JOIN drivers AS d ON d.driver_id = sub.driver_id
WHERE sub.rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Constructor standings rows for the final race of each season
SELECT r.year,
       cs.constructor_id,
       cs.points
FROM constructor_standings AS cs
JOIN races AS r ON r.race_id = cs.race_id
WHERE r.round = (
        SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Rank constructors by points and keep the top one per year
SELECT *
FROM (
    SELECT r.year,
           cs.constructor_id,
           cs.points,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.year ORDER BY cs.points DESC) AS rn
    FROM constructor_standings cs
    JOIN races r ON r.race_id = cs.race_id
    WHERE r.round = ( SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year )
) AS sub
WHERE rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Attach the constructor name to each seasonal winner row
SELECT sub.year,
       c.name  AS constructor_name,
       sub.points
FROM (
    SELECT r.year,
           cs.constructor_id,
           cs.points,
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.year ORDER BY cs.points DESC) AS rn
    FROM constructor_standings cs
    JOIN races r ON r.race_id = cs.race_id
    WHERE r.round = ( SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year )
) AS sub
JOIN constructors AS c ON c.constructor_id = sub.constructor_id
WHERE sub.rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Bring the two previous result sets together: champion driver + champion constructor for every season
SELECT d.year,
       d.full_name         AS champion_driver,
       c.constructor_name  AS champion_constructor
FROM (
    SELECT sub.year,
           drv.full_name,
           sub.points
    FROM (
        SELECT r.year,
               ds.driver_id,
               ds.points,
               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.year ORDER BY ds.points DESC) AS rn
        FROM driver_standings ds
        JOIN races r ON r.race_id = ds.race_id
        WHERE r.round = ( SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year )
    ) AS sub
    JOIN drivers drv ON drv.driver_id = sub.driver_id
    WHERE sub.rn = 1
) AS d
JOIN (
    SELECT sub.year,
           cons.name AS constructor_name,
           sub.points
    FROM (
        SELECT r.year,
               cs.constructor_id,
               cs.points,
               ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY r.year ORDER BY cs.points DESC) AS rn
        FROM constructor_standings cs
        JOIN races r ON r.race_id = cs.race_id
        WHERE r.round = ( SELECT MAX(r2.round) FROM races r2 WHERE r2.year = r.year )
    ) AS sub
    JOIN constructors cons ON cons.constructor_id = sub.constructor_id
    WHERE sub.rn = 1
) AS c
  ON c.year = d.year
ORDER BY d.year
LIMIT 20;
```
